-- title:
--   Local U_ℓ set: ℓ cosets and one-sided indices ℓ
-- statement:
--   Fix $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and $\mathbb{Z}$-submodules $R,\Lambda$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$, and let $N$ be a non-zero squarefree natural number such that $R=\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_2$ for two maximal orders (orders being submodules containing $1$, closed under multiplication, spanning $\mathbb{H}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and finitely generated, maximal among such) with $[\Lambda_1:R]=N$ as additive groups, while $\Lambda$ is a maximal order and $R\le\Lambda$. Let $\ell$ be a prime dividing $N$ and $q'$ a prime with $q'\neq\ell$, $q'\nmid N$, such that $a<0$, $b<0$ and, for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, every non-zero element of $\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a unit exactly when $q'\in v$. Let $v$ be a height-one prime containing $\ell$, and write $R_v,\Lambda_v$ for the additive subgroups of $\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ generated by the $z\otimes c$ with $z$ in $R$, resp. $\Lambda$, and $c$ in the valuation ring, and $K$ for the stabiliser of $R_v$ under the pointwise action of $(\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v)^\times$. Call a unit $g$ admissible if $g\in R_v$, $\ell g^{-1}\in R_v$, $g^{-1}\notin R_v$, $\ell^{-1}g\notin R_v$, the map $z\mapsto g^{-1}zg$ does not satisfy $g^{-1}zg\in R_v\iff z\in R_v$ for all $z$, and does not send $R_v$ into $\Lambda_v$. Then the set of classes in $(\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v)^\times/K$ represented by admissible units has exactly $\ell$ elements, and for every admissible $g$ both $[K:K\cap gKg^{-1}]$ and $[K:K\cap g^{-1}Kg]$, in the form of the relative index of $K\sqcap \mathrm{conj}_{g^{\pm1}}(K)$ in $K$, equal $\ell$.
--
--   This is the local computation at a prime $\ell$ dividing the squarefree level underlying the degree of the Hecke operator $U_\ell$ for an Eichler order: in a splitting carrying $\Lambda_v$ to $M_2(\mathbb{Z}_\ell)$ and $R_v$ to the Iwahori order, the admissible units form the single double coset of $\mathrm{diag}(\ell,1)$, with its $\ell$ one-sided cosets and one-sided indices $\ell$. It feeds the computation of stabiliser indices along the Hecke correspondences in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development.
